\paragraph{$\aeff$ as a proxy for true $\alpha$.}
The method's effectiveness depends on $\aeff$ tracking the true batch-level real fraction.
With the calibrated TF-IDF detector, $\aeff$ correlates at Pearson $r \approx 0.70$ across batch sizes 4, 8, and 16 (Appendix~\ref{app:alpha_validation}), with a consistent conservative bias of $-0.03$: the proxy slightly underestimates the true real fraction, biasing the method toward less intervention.
At $B{=}4$, $\aeff$ is discretized into five values (0, 0.25, 0.5, 0.75, 1), yet the correlation is not degraded relative to larger batch sizes, because batch-level aggregation smooths sample-level noise.

