\paragraph{Why not a fixed $b$?}
Training with the aggregate mean $\bstar \approx 1.4$ as a fixed exponent yields PPL 17.693, no better than uniform (17.694), because the average masks extreme per-batch heterogeneity (std $\approx$ 1.9, range $[0, 20]$): it over-penalizes the 55\% of batches needing mild correction ($\bstar < 1$) and under-penalizes the 3.6\% requiring aggressive intervention ($\bstar > 5$).
The grid-searched optimum ($b{=}0.3$ at $\rho{=}0.4$, $b{=}0.5$ at $\rho{=}0.6$) varies with contamination level, requiring per-setting retuning that the adaptive method eliminates.

tool_args.new_string:
\paragraph{Why not a fixed $b$?}
The aggregate mean $\bstar \approx 1.4$ as a fixed exponent yields PPL 17.693, no better than uniform, because it masks extreme per-batch heterogeneity (std $\approx$ 1.9, range $[0, 20]$).
The grid optimum ($b{=}0.3$ at $\rho{=}0.4$, $b{=}0.5$ at $\rho{=}0.6$) varies with contamination level; the adaptive method eliminates this per-setting retuning.

\paragraph{$\tau$ as a practical dial.}
$\tau \in [0.5, 0.8]$ all outperform uniform (Table~\ref{tab:tau}), with asymmetric sensitivity: over-cleaning ($\tau > 0.8$) degrades sharply as $\bstar$ inflates, while under-cleaning is mild.
We recommend $\tau = 0.8$ as default; $\tau \in [0.7, 0.8]$ is robust across $\rho \in \{0.4, 0.6\}$ (Appendix~\ref{app:tau_rho}).
